  // design: St. John's step 8b (the owner's photo of Memorial Hall: a many-sided two-storey body, fieldstone with pale-surrounded square windows and a pale band course below, dark red-brown shingles with a band of wide cream-framed windows above, under a tall steep faceted shingle roof with a flared eave and a small metal cap, a lower roof over the long body behind, a big pale arch in the ground storey's wall beside the faceted end; docs/map-briefs/st_johns). The octagon's whole square, 16 by 16 m: its south half is the Library Bay (two storeys) turned so the hall runs north-south (its parts, part for part; Arch porch: west or east), its north half the Faceted Hall End (spiral stair) tile. The Faceted Spire's hall variant stands on the walls' top centred on the octagon's centre (the middle of the square), cut away where the bay's Ridge Roof Bay runs in under it: inside, the two roofs are one space. A square Leaded Window in every face of the ground storey (two in the north face, one in each diagonal face and each side wall); two wide Leaded Windows in each of the three upper faces and one in each side wall, close under the eave. The stair's drum stands in the middle of the end: the hall's aisle and the runner upstairs lead to its door on both floors, a passage 4 m wide goes round it each side on the ground floor (a rug and a lantern in each), the upper floor is open to the spire. Turns only.
